<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mentuhotep IV</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 1990 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century-bce">20th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mentuhotep IV was the last king of the Eleventh Dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, reigning for a few years around 1990 BCE and omitted from later king lists. He is known mainly from inscriptions in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wadi-hammamat">Wadi Hammamat</a>, where his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> Amenemhat led a large quarrying expedition. That vizier is usually identified with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amenemhat-i">Amenemhat I</a>, founder of the Twelfth Dynasty.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mentuhotep-iv.jpg" alt="Mentuhotep IV" width="198" height="297" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Karl Richard Lepsius (1810-84) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Relief_Mentuhotep_IV_Lepsius.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
